  "summary": "New analyses show AUC misses diagnosis timing and same-modality validation inflates miRNA classifiers, while a calibrated epigenetic clock targets a regional population.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that conventional evaluation can misrepresent clinical model utility. The mammography preprint reports that AUC and C-index for breast cancer risk models are insensitive to diagnosis timing, and proposes a two-stage whether-versus-when model. The glioblastoma circulating miRNA preprint tests CTGAN and TVAE augmentation and finds that same-modality validation overstates performance, with cross-platform AUROC around 0.40-0.47, near chance. The published epigenetic clock study addresses a related issue of population specificity: a SuperLearner trained on non-Nicoyan Costa Ricans is used to calibrate age acceleration for the Nicoya blue zone. Together the items point to timing, cross-platform transfer and population calibration as properties that discrimination metrics alone do not capture. Two of the three items are preprints, so those findings remain provisional.",
